  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A stalling cold front may bring rain at times \nfrom Friday through Sunday.\n\nA cold front will approach from the northwest late Friday \nafternoon into Friday evening before eventually pushing south \nof the area Friday night. Showers will likely reach western \nMaryland by the afternoon, progresses southeastward during the \nevening and into the night. Despite the warm temperatures, poor\nlapse rates aloft will limit instability, so few if any \nthunderstorms are expected. A westerly component to the flow \ncould also result in downslope drying, and many models show \nshowers becoming more scattered in nature with eastward extent. \nRainfall amounts with this round will likely be less than a \nquarter inch for most locations.\n\nStrong high pressure will pass north of the area Saturday while\nthe front stalls across the southeastern states. Southern stream\nmoisture will likely overrun the boundary fairly far to the\nnorth, but there remains some uncertainty in the dividing line\nbetween rain and no rain. Overall, Saturday probably has a\nbetter chance of being dry, especially the further north you go \nwith strong high pressure set up to the north. \n\nA wave of low pressure will likely ride along the boundary \nSaturday night into Sunday, and this may provide a higher chance\nof rain spreading northward into the area. Latest guidance has \nshifted maintained the northward shift with rainfall coverage \nduring this period. In the end, the details are far from \nsolidified. However, any rain that does occur will likely exit \nthe area Sunday evening.\n\nBeing north of the boundary and potentially having a lot of\ncloud cover, high temperatures will likely remain in the 60s for\nmost areas over the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... First widespread frost and freeze potential west of \nthe Blue Ridge early next week.\n\nThe true Fall feel sets in next week with highs and lows near to \nbelow normal. Monday will feature broad 1025-1028 mb high pressure \nbuilding east from the upper Midwest/western Great Lakes behind a \ndeparting upper trof/secondary cold front exiting the Mid-Atlantic \ncoast. The frontal passage itself appears dry with perhaps a \nlingering shower/sprinkle if any moisture is leftover. Breezy west \nto northwest winds will accompany the front with gusts 20-25 mph and \nhigh temperatures in the upper 60s and mid 70s (upper 50s mountains).\n\nHigh pressure moves overhead Monday night into Tuesday allowing the \nwinds to lighten and skies to clear. This will yield a prime \nradiational cooling setup, especially west of the Blue Ridge where \nwidespread frost and freeze conditions are possible. Of particular \nconcern is the high elevations and sheltered valleys of which could \ndrop down to around freezing. Elsewhere, expect lows in the upper \n30s and mid 40s. Locations near the water will be in the low 50s. \nSimilar conditions are expected Tuesday night with widespread mid \nto upper 30s west of the Blue and north/west of the I-95 metro \nareas. These chilly conditions (concern for frost) is roughly 1 to 2 \nweeks earlier than the average time for this time of year. \n\nOutside of the frost and freeze potential, dry conditions will \nprevail for much of the week with broad high pressure overhead. A \ncold front looks to cross the region Thursday into Friday, perhaps \nbringing a few showers to mountains. Otherwise, another large area \nof high pressure returns for the weekend with warming temperatures \nheading into the following workweek. Highs Tuesday through Wednesday \nwill likely be in the 60s (50s mountains), with lows in the 40s to \nnear 50 (30s mountains). Thursday and Friday highs warm back into \nthe upper 60s and mid 70s (low 60s mountains) with lows in the 40s \nand 50s.",
